<%!--
  A stream subscription's checkpoint, written only while the stream its position is in, by
  `stream_id`, still exists (sparkle-space/sparklespace#8386). After a hard delete of the
  stream, an ack of an event of the deleted stream writes nothing, so it cannot put back a
  position the hard delete reset (`reset_stream_subscriptions`). It answers whether the
  stream was found and whether the row was written: a stream not found means the stream
  was hard deleted (and maybe created again, under a new `stream_id`), and a row not
  written beside a stream found means the row is gone.

  The stream's row is taken FOR KEY SHARE first, in `stream`, which the UPDATE's condition
  reads, so the lock is taken before the subscription row's. A locking read, unlike a plain
  one, sees a row deleted by a transaction that committed while it waited: a hard delete
  that has deleted the row makes it wait, and then it finds no row, whatever its snapshot
  saw. KEY SHARE does not conflict with an append's NO KEY UPDATE, so appends never wait on
  a checkpoint, and a checkpoint waits only on the DELETE of the stream's row, the lock a
  versioned snapshot write takes too (`lock_snapshot_stream`). The hard delete resets the
  subscription rows after it deletes the stream's row, so it never holds a subscription
  row this write waits on while waiting on this write's KEY SHARE.

  `stream` is read twice and materialised once, so both reads see the one locking read.
--%>
WITH stream AS (
  SELECT stream_id
  FROM "<%= schema %>".streams
  WHERE stream_id = $3
  FOR KEY SHARE
),
acked AS (
  UPDATE "<%= schema %>".subscriptions
  SET last_seen = GREATEST(COALESCE(last_seen, 0), $2)
  WHERE subscription_id = $1
    AND EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM stream)
  RETURNING subscription_id
)
SELECT
  EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM stream),
  EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM acked);
